Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from the book by Charles Dickens
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NARRATOR'S STUDY - NIGHT

A modest Victorian study. A fire crackles. NARRATOR, in a dressing gown, sits in an armchair with an old book open on his lap. He looks directly at camera.

NARRATOR
Marley was dead, to begin with. There is no doubt whatever about that. Scrooge knew he was dead. Of course he did. Scrooge and he were partners.

The NARRATOR closes the book. The fire flares.

NARRATOR (cont'd)
This must be distinctly understood, or nothing wonderful can come of the story I am going to relate.

INT. COUNTING HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE - AFTERNOON

A cold, bleak office. Fog presses at the windows. SCROOGE, hard and sharp as flint, with frosty rime on his head, pores over his books. In the dismal tank beyond, BOB CRATCHIT hunches over letters, trying to warm himself at a candle, white comforter around his neck.

The door bursts open. FRED, Scrooge's nephew, enters ruddy and glowing from the cold.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
I do. Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Don't be cross, uncle.

SCROOGE
What else can I be, when I live in such a world of fools as this? Out upon merry Christmas! If I could work my will, every idiot who goes about with "Merry Christmas" on his lips should be boiled with his own pudding and buried with a stake of holly through his heart.

FRED
Uncle!

SCROOGE
Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
But you don't keep it.

SCROOGE
Let me leave it alone, then. Much good may it do you!

FRED
There are many things from which I might have derived good by which I have not profited. Christmas among the rest. I have always thought of Christmas time as a good time, a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time. And therefore, uncle, though it has never put a scrap of gold or silver in my pocket, I believe that it has done me good and will do me good, and I say God bless it!

BOB CRATCHIT involuntarily applauds from the tank, then pokes the fire in embarrassment.

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Don't be angry, uncle. Come! Dine with us tomorrow.

SCROOGE
I'll see you in that extremity first.

FRED
Why?

SCROOGE
Why did you get married?

FRED
Because I fell in love.

SCROOGE
Because you fell in love! Good afternoon!

FRED
I want nothing from you. Why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED
A Merry Christmas, uncle!

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

Fred leaves without anger. He stops at the outer door to greet Bob cordially.

SCROOGE
There's another fellow, my clerk with fifteen shillings a week and a wife and family, talking about a merry Christmas. I'll retire to Bedlam.

Two PORTLY GENTLEMEN enter with books and papers.

MR. HOPKINS
Scrooge and Marley's, I believe. Have I the pleasure of addressing Mr. Scrooge or Mr. Marley?

SCROOGE
Mr. Marley has been dead these seven years. He died seven years ago this very night.

MR. HOPKINS
We have no doubt his liberality is well represented by his surviving partner.

The gentlemen present credentials. Scrooge frowns at the word "liberality" and hands them back.

MR. HOPKINS (cont'd)
At this festive season, Mr. Scrooge, it is more than usually desirable that we should make some slight provision for the Poor and destitute who suffer greatly at the present time. What shall I put you down for?

SCROOGE
Nothing!

MR. HOPKINS
You wish to be anonymous?

SCROOGE
I wish to be left alone. I don't make merry myself at Christmas and I can't afford to make idle people merry. I help to support the prisons and the Union workhouses -- they cost enough -- and those who are badly off must go there.

MR. HOPKINS
Many can't go there, and many would rather die.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it and decrease the surplus population. Good afternoon, gentlemen!

The gentlemen withdraw. Scrooge resumes work, pleased with himself.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Foggier yet, and colder. Piercing, searching, biting cold.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

A gloomy suite in a lowering pile of buildings. Fog and frost cling to the black gateway. SCROOGE approaches the door with his key. The large knocker transforms into MARLEY'S FACE -- dismal, with ghostly spectacles turned up on its forehead, hair stirred as if by hot air, livid color.

SCROOGE starts, then shakes it off.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

He enters, lights a candle, checks every room and closet, double-locks himself in. He sits by a very low fire in his dressing-gown and nightcap, eating gruel. The tiles around the fireplace seem to show Marley's face on every one.

SCROOGE
Humbug!

A disused bell begins to swing and ring loudly. Every bell in the house joins. A clanking noise rises from below -- heavy chains dragged over casks. The cellar door booms. The noise climbs the stairs.

SCROOGE
It's humbug still! I won't believe it.

The door flies open. MARLEY'S GHOST enters -- pigtail, waistcoat, tights, boots, the chain of cash-boxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds and heavy purses wound about him like a tail. His body is transparent. The dying flame leaps up, cries "I know him! Marley's Ghost!" and falls.

SCROOGE
How now! What do you want with me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Much!

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY'S GHOST
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
Can you -- can you sit down?

MARLEY'S GHOST
I can.

The Ghost sits opposite.

MARLEY'S GHOST (cont'd)
You don't believe in me.

SCROOGE
I don't.

MARLEY'S GHOST
Why do you doubt your senses?

SCROOGE
Because a little thing affects them. A slight disorder of the stomach makes them cheats. You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese, a fragment of an underdone potato. There's more of gravy than of grave about you, whatever you are!

The Ghost raises a frightful cry and shakes its chain. It removes the bandage from its head; its lower jaw drops upon its breast.

SCROOGE falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
Man of the worldly mind! Do you believe in me or not?

SCROOGE
I do. I must. But why do spirits walk the earth, and why do they come to me?

MARLEY'S GHOST
It is required of every man that the spirit within him should walk abroad among his fellow-men. If that spirit goes not forth in life, it is condemned to do so after death. I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link and yard by yard. Is its pattern strange to you?

SCROOGE trembles, glances at the floor expecting his own chain.

MARLEY'S GHOST (cont'd)
I have none to give. I cannot rest, I cannot stay, I cannot linger anywhere. My spirit never walked beyond our counting-house in life. Weary journeys lie before me!

The Ghost cries out again, shakes its chain, wrings its shadowy hands.

MARLEY'S GHOST (cont'd)
At this time of the rolling year I suffer most. Mankind was my business. The common welfare was my business. Charity, mercy, forbearance and benevolence were all my business. The dealings of my trade were but a drop of water in the comprehensive ocean of my business!

It holds up its chain, flings it upon the ground.

MARLEY'S GHOST (cont'd)
You will be haunted by Three Spirits. Expect the first tomorrow when the bell tolls One. Expect the second on the next night at the same hour. The third upon the next night when the last stroke of Twelve has ceased to vibrate. Look to see me no more. Remember what has passed between us!

The Ghost binds its jaw with the wrapper, walks backward. The window raises itself. It beckons. Scrooge approaches. The air fills with confused noises of lamentation. The Ghost joins the mournful dirge and floats out upon the bleak dark night. Phantoms wander in chains outside the window, moaning.

SCROOGE closes the window. The door is still double-locked. He tries to say "Humbug" but cannot. Exhausted, he falls into bed and sleeps instantly.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The bell strikes one. Light flashes. The bed curtains are drawn aside by a hand. SCROOGE starts up. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands before him -- like a child yet like an old man, white hair, unwrinkled face, bare arms and legs, tunic of purest white, belt of lustrous sheen, holly branch in hand trimmed with summer flowers. From its head springs a bright clear jet of light. It carries an extinguisher cap under its arm. The figure fluctuates in distinctness.

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit, sir, whose coming was foretold to me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
I am. I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your past.

The Ghost clasps Scrooge gently by the arm.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST (cont'd)
Rise! And walk with me!

They pass through the wall.

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY (PAST)

Snow on the ground. Clear cold winter day. The city has vanished. SCROOGE clasps his hands.

SCROOGE
Good Heaven! I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Your lip is trembling. What is that upon your cheek?

SCROOGE
It is a pimple.

They walk. A market-town appears with bridge, church, river. Boys on ponies shout "Merry Christmas!"

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST (cont'd)
These are but shadows of the things that have been. They have no consciousness of us.

They approach a dull red brick mansion, broken fortunes, fowls in the stables. They enter a long bare melancholy room. A lonely boy reads by a feeble fire. It is young SCROOGE.

SCROOGE
Poor boy!

The Ghost points. A man in foreign garments appears outside the window with an ass laden with wood.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
Why, it's Ali Baba! It's dear old honest Ali Baba! And Valentine and his wild brother Orson! And the Sultan's Groom turned upside down by the Genii! And the Parrot! Poor Robin Crusoe! Halloa! Hoop! Halloo!

SCROOGE weeps.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
I wish... but it's too late now.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
Let us see another Christmas!

The room darkens, grows dirtier. Young SCROOGE walks despairingly. A little girl, FAN, much younger, darts in, embraces him.

FAN
Dear, dear brother! I have come to bring you home, home, home! Father is so much kinder than he used to be. Home, for good and all. And you're to be a man and never come back here, but first we're to be together all the Christmas long and have the merriest time in all the world.

They exit joyfully. The schoolmaster shakes hands ferociously.

The scene dissolves. They stand at a warehouse door.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST (cont'd)
You recollect the way?

SCROOGE
Was I apprenticed here!

They enter. OLD FEZZIWIG sits behind a high desk.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
Why, it's old Fezziwig! Bless his heart, it's Fezziwig alive again!

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick!

Young SCROOGE and DICK WILKINS enter briskly.

FEZZIWIG (cont'd)
No more work tonight. Christmas Eve, Dick. Christmas, Ebenezer! Let's have the shutters up before a man can say Jack Robinson!

The apprentices clear the floor in a twinkling. A fiddler appears. MRS. FEZZIWIG, the three MISS FEZZIWIGS, young followers, employees, housemaid, baker, cook, milkman, boy from over the way -- all enter. They dance "Sir Roger de Coverley." Fezziwig and Mrs. Fezziwig lead, cutting deftly.

At eleven the ball ends. Fezziwig and wife wish everyone a Merry Christmas.

SCROOGE
It isn't that, Spirit. He has the power to render us happy or unhappy. The happiness he gives is quite as great as if it cost a fortune.

The Ghost smiles. The scene changes again. Young SCROOGE, now a man with signs of avarice, sits beside a fair young girl in mourning, BELLE. Tears sparkle in her eyes.

BELLE
It matters little. To you, very little. Another idol has displaced me. A golden one.

SCROOGE
This is the even-handed dealing of the world! There is nothing it professes to condemn with such severity as the pursuit of wealth!

BELLE
You fear the world too much. I have seen your nobler aspirations fall off one by one until the master-passion, Gain, engrosses you. Our contract is an old one. You are changed. I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were. May you be happy in the life you have chosen!

She leaves. SCROOGE turns on the Ghost.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Show me no more! Conduct me home. Why do you delight to torture me?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST
One shadow more!

They are in a comfortable room. BELLE, now a comely matron, sits laughing with her daughter and many children. A husband enters with toys. The children swarm him. Belle and husband sit by the fire.

HUSBAND
I saw an old friend of yours this afternoon.

BELLE
Mr. Scrooge.

HUSBAND
His partner lies upon the point of death, I hear, and there he sat alone. Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Remove me from this place. I cannot bear it!

He wrestles with the Ghost, seizes the extinguisher cap and presses it down upon its head. The light streams out but the Ghost sinks beneath it. Scrooge, exhausted, reels to bed and sleeps.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

SCROOGE awakes in the middle of a snore. The bell strikes one again. He parts the curtains himself, ready. A blaze of ruddy light fills the room. Nothing appears. He shuffles to the door.

A strange voice calls him.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT (O.S.)
Enter!

He obeys. The room is transformed into a perfect grove of living green, holly, mistletoe, ivy. A mighty blaze roars in the chimney. Heaped like a throne are turkeys, geese, game, brawn, mince-pies, plum-puddings, barrels of oysters, fruits, twelfth-cakes, bowls of punch steaming.

Upon the couch sits the GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT -- a jolly Giant, green robe bordered with white fur, bare breast and feet, holly wreath with icicles, long free brown curls, sparkling eye, open hand, cheery voice. An antique scabbard without a sword, eaten with rust. He holds a glowing torch like Plenty's horn.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in! And know me better, man! I am the Ghost of Christmas Present. Look upon me!

SCROOGE does so reverently.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT (cont'd)
Touch my robe!

They stand in the city streets on Christmas morning. People shovel snow, exchange snowballs, laugh. Shops glow with holly, fruits, meats. Grocers' windows radiant. People hurry with dinners to bakers' ovens. The Ghost sprinkles incense from his torch on the dinners, restoring good humor where quarrels arise.

SCROOGE
Is there a peculiar flavour in what you sprinkle from your torch?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There is. My own. To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

They go invisible to the suburbs, to BOB CRATCHIT'S four-roomed house. The Ghost blesses it with his torch.

Inside, MRS. CRATCHIT, brave in ribbons, lays the cloth with BELINDA. MASTER PETER in his monstrous collar mashes potatoes. The two young CRATCHITS scream they smell the goose. MARTHA arrives late from work.

They bustle. BOB CRATCHIT enters with TINY TIM upon his shoulder. Tiny Tim bears a little crutch, limbs supported by iron frame.

BOB CRATCHIT
Why, where's our Martha?

The children hide Martha then reveal her. They prepare the goose. Grace is said. Mrs. Cratchit carves. The stuffing gushes. Tiny Tim beats the table with his knife.

TINY TIM
Hurrah!

There never was such a goose. They eat. The pudding arrives blazing in brandy and holly. Universal admiration. They draw round the hearth. Chestnuts crackle. Bob serves hot mixture from a jug.

BOB CRATCHIT
A Merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

ALL
God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

SCROOGE
Spirit, tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner and a crutch without an owner, carefully preserved. If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE
No, no! Oh no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, none other of my race will find him here. What then? If he be like to die, he had better do it and decrease the surplus population.

SCROOGE hangs his head, hearing his own words.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT (cont'd)
Man, if man you be in heart, not adamant, forbear that wicked cant until you have discovered What the surplus is, and Where it is. Will you decide what men shall live, what men shall die?

The Ghost turns on him with his own words again.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT (cont'd)
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

They travel on. They visit a bleak moor with miners singing Christmas songs by fire. Then a lighthouse where keepers wish Merry Christmas in grog and sing. Then a ship at sea where every man hums a Christmas tune or speaks kindly of home.

They stand in FRED'S bright room. FRED laughs heartily.

FRED
Ha, ha! He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live! He believed it too!

FRED'S NIECE
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
He's a comical old fellow. His offences carry their own punishment. I am sorry for him. I couldn't be angry with him if I tried. Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always. I mean to give him the same chance every year. He may rail at Christmas till he dies, but he can't help thinking better of it if he finds me going there in good temper year after year and saying "Uncle Scrooge, how are you?" If it only puts him in the vein to leave his poor clerk fifty pounds, that's something.

They play Yes and No. Fred thinks of an animal that is disagreeable, savage, growls and grunts, lives in London, walks the streets, is not led by anyone, not in a menagerie. They guess.

MISS LUCY
It's your Uncle Scro-o-o-o-oge!

They drink his health with merriment. The Ghost smiles approvingly at Scrooge.

They see many homes, sickbeds made cheerful, struggling men patient, poverty made rich. The Ghost grows older, hair grey.

SCROOGE
Are spirits' lives so short?

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT
My life upon this globe is very brief. It ends tonight.

From the Ghost's robe come two children -- a boy and girl, yellow, meagre, ragged, wolfish, prostrate. Ignorance and Want.

GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT (cont'd)
Oh, Man! Look here. Look, look down here! This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want. Beware them both, and all of their degree, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom, unless the writing be erased. Deny it! Slander those who tell it ye! Admit it for your factious purposes and make it worse. And bide the end!

The bell strikes twelve. The Ghost vanishes. A solemn Phantom, draped and hooded in black, comes like a mist along the ground.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

SCROOGE falls to his knee. The GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME scatters gloom. It is tall, stately, silent, one outstretched hand visible. It points onward.

SCROOGE
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas Yet To Come? You are about to show me shadows of the things that have not happened but will happen in the time before us. Is that so, Spirit?

The upper fold contracts as if nodding.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
Lead on! The night is waning fast and it is precious time to me. Lead on, Spirit!

They seem to spring up in the heart of the city on 'Change. Merchants converse.

MR. BULKLEY
I don't know much about it, either way. I only know he's dead.

MR. PECK
When did he die?

MR. BULKLEY
Last night, I believe.

MR. GRUBB
God knows. What has he done with his money?

MR. BULKLEY
Left it to his company, perhaps. He hasn't left it to me. It's likely to be a very cheap funeral. I don't know of anybody to go to it. Suppose we make up a party and volunteer?

They laugh and stroll away. The Ghost points to two other businessmen.

MR. SMYTHE
Old Scratch has got his own at last, hey?

MR. HALE
So I am told. Cold, isn't it? Good morning!

The Ghost glides into an obscure part of town, foul narrow ways, wretched shops and people. They enter a low-browed shop dealing in rusty keys, nails, chains, old rags, bottles, bones. An old grey-haired rascal of nearly seventy smokes his pipe by a charcoal stove.

A woman with a heavy bundle slinks in, then another, then a man in faded black. They recognize each other and laugh.

MRS. DILBER
Let the charwoman alone to be the first! Let the laundress alone to be the second, and let the undertaker's man alone to be the third. If we haven't all three met here without meaning it!

OLD JOE
You couldn't have met in a better place. Come into the parlour.

They enter the space behind a screen of rags. The first woman throws her bundle down.

MRS. DILBER
Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did.

They produce plunder: seals, pencil-case, sleeve-buttons, brooch, sheets, towels, silver teaspoons, sugar-tongs, boots, bed-curtains rings and all, blankets, the dead man's best shirt.

MRS. DILBER
Why wasn't he natural in his lifetime? If he had been, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death instead of lying gasping out his last there, alone by himself. It's a judgment on him.

They laugh. Old Joe pays them in coins from a flannel bag.

MRS. DILBER (cont'd)
This is the end of it, you see! He frightened every one away from him when he was alive to profit us when he was dead! Ha, ha, ha!

SCROOGE shudders.

SCROOGE
Spirit! I see, I see. The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way now. Merciful Heaven, what is this!

The scene changes. They stand by a bare uncurtained bed. A pale light falls on it. Beneath a ragged sheet lies a something covered up -- the plundered body of the dead man. The room is dark, unwatched, unwept, uncared for. A cat tears at the door. Rats gnaw beneath the hearth.

The Ghost's hand points to the head. The cover could be raised with a finger's motion.

SCROOGE
Spirit, this is a fearful place. In leaving it I shall not leave its lesson, trust me. Let us go!

The hand remains pointed.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
I understand you, and I would do it if I could. But I have not the power.

He agonizes.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me, Spirit, I beseech you!

The Ghost spreads its robe like a wing, reveals a room by daylight. A mother and children wait anxiously. The husband enters, careworn but with serious delight he struggles to repress.

HUSBAND
Bad.

WIFE
We are quite ruined?

HUSBAND
No. There is hope yet, Caroline. He is dead.

WIFE
He is past relenting. He was not only very ill but dying then.

They soften. Their hearts are lighter. The children brighten. It is a happier house for this man's death.

SCROOGE
Let me see some tenderness connected with a death, or that dark chamber will be for ever present to me.

The Ghost conducts him to BOB CRATCHIT'S house. Quiet. The family sits round the fire. MRS. CRATCHIT and daughters sew. PETER reads. The little ones are still as statues.

PETER
"'And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them.'"

MRS. CRATCHIT puts her hand to her face.

MRS. CRATCHIT
The colour hurts my eyes.

She composes herself.

MRS. CRATCHIT (cont'd)
It makes them weak by candle-light, and I wouldn't show weak eyes to your father when he comes home for the world. It must be near his time.

BOB enters with his comforter. They help him. The children press close, saying without words not to mind it. Bob is cheerful, praises the sewing.

BOB CRATCHIT
Sunday! You went today then, Robert?

MRS. CRATCHIT
Yes, my dear. I wish you could have gone. It would have done you good to see how green a place it is. But you'll see it often. I promised him that I would walk there on a Sunday. My little, little child!

Bob breaks down, then composes himself, kisses the little face upstairs in the room hung with Christmas where a chair stands close beside. He returns reconciled, happy.

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very happy.

They kiss and shake hands. The family vows never to forget Tiny Tim.

SCROOGE
Spectre, something informs me that our parting moment is at hand. Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead?

The Ghost conveys him to his own place of occupation. It is not his. Another man sits in the chair. The Ghost points onward to a churchyard, walled in by houses, overrun by grass and weeds, choked with too much burying.

The Spirit stands among the graves and points down to one. SCROOGE advances trembling.

SCROOGE
Before I draw nearer to that stone to which you point, answer me one question. Are these the shadows of the things that Will be, or are they shadows of things that May be only?

The Ghost points immovable to the grave. SCROOGE creeps to it, reads his own name upon the stone.

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The finger points from grave to him and back.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
No, Spirit! Oh no, no!

The finger is still there.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
Spirit! Hear me! I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse. Why show me this if I am past all hope?

The hand appears to shake.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
Good Spirit, your nature intercedes for me and pities me. Assure me that I yet may change these shadows you have shown me by an altered life!

The hand trembles.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
I will honour Christmas in my heart and try to keep it all the year. I will live in the Past, the Present and the Future. The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. I will not shut out the lessons that they teach. Oh, tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

He catches the hand in agony. The Spirit repulses him. Scrooge falls to his knees in last prayer. The Phantom's hood and dress shrink, collapse, dwindle into a bedpost.

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - CHRISTMAS MORNING

SCROOGE scrambles out of bed. The bedpost is his own. The bed and room are his own.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present and the Future! The Spirits of all Three shall strive within me. Oh Jacob Marley! Heaven and the Christmas Time be praised for this!

He is fluttered, glowing, sobbing with joy. He folds the bed-curtains.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
They are not torn down, rings and all. They are here -- I am here -- the shadows of the things that would have been may be dispelled. They will be. I know they will!

He dresses in his best, laughing and crying. He frisks into the sitting-room.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
There's the saucepan that the gruel was in! There's the door by which the Ghost of Jacob Marley entered! There's the corner where the Ghost of Christmas Present sat! There's the window where I saw the wandering Spirits! It's all right, it's all true, it all happened. Ha ha ha!

He laughs splendidly. The churches ring out lusty peals. He opens the window. Clear bright jovial cold, golden sunlight, merry bells.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
What's today?

A BOY in Sunday clothes looks up.

BOY
EH?

SCROOGE
What's today, my fine fellow?

BOY
Today! Why, CHRISTMAS DAY.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it. The Spirits have done it all in one night. They can do anything they like. Hallo, my fine fellow!

BOY
Hallo!

SCROOGE
Do you know the Poulterer's in the next street but one at the corner?

BOY
I should hope I did.

SCROOGE
An intelligent boy! A remarkable boy! Do you know whether they've sold the prize Turkey that was hanging up there? Not the little prize Turkey, the big one?

BOY
What, the one as big as me?

SCROOGE
What a delightful boy! It's a pleasure to talk to him. Yes, my buck!

BOY
It's hanging there now.

SCROOGE
Is it? Go and buy it. Tell 'em to bring it here that I may give them the direction where to take it. Come back with the man and I'll give you a shilling. Come back with him in less than five minutes and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The boy shoots off. Scrooge rubs his hands, splits with laughter.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
I'll send it to Bob Cratchit's! He shan't know who sends it. It's twice the size of Tiny Tim.

He writes the address with shaking hand, opens the street door. He pats the knocker.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
I shall love it as long as I live! I scarcely ever looked at it before. What an honest expression it has in its face! It's a wonderful knocker!

The Turkey arrives. Scrooge chuckles paying for it, the cab, the boy. He sits breathless, chuckling till he cries.

He dresses, goes into the streets. People pour forth. He regards everyone with a delighted smile. Three or four fellows greet him.

PASSERSBY
Good morning, sir! A merry Christmas to you!

He meets one of the PORTLY GENTLEMEN from yesterday.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, how do you do? I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. A merry Christmas to you, sir!

MR. HOPKINS
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE
Yes. That is my name, and I fear it may not be pleasant to you. Allow me to ask your pardon. And will you have the goodness --

He whispers in the gentleman's ear.

MR. HOPKINS
Lord bless me! My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
If you please. Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it. Will you do me that favour?

MR. HOPKINS
My dear sir, I don't know what to say to such munifi--

SCROOGE
Don't say anything, please. Come and see me. Will you come and see me?

MR. HOPKINS
I will!

SCROOGE
Thank'ee. I am much obliged to you. I thank you fifty times. Bless you!

He goes to church, walks the streets, pats children, questions beggars, looks into kitchens and windows. Everything yields him pleasure. In the afternoon he turns toward his nephew's house. He passes the door a dozen times before knocking.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - AFTERNOON

The table is spread. FRED and his wife look at it nervously. SCROOGE sidles his face round the door.

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

Fred lets him in heartily. The niece, Topper, the plump sister, all greet him. Wonderful party, wonderful games, wonderful happiness.

INT. COUNTING HOUSE - NEXT MORNING

SCROOGE is early. The clock strikes nine. No Bob. Quarter past. Bob is eighteen and a half minutes late. He enters, hat and comforter off, on his stool driving his pen.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB CRATCHIT
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Yes. I think you are. Step this way, sir, if you please.

Bob appears from the tank.

BOB CRATCHIT
It's only once a year, sir. It shall not be repeated. I was making rather merry yesterday, sir.

SCROOGE
Now, I'll tell you what, my friend, I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer. And therefore --

He leaps from his stool, digs Bob in the waistcoat.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
I am about to raise your salary!

Bob trembles, eyes the ruler.

SCROOGE (cont'd)
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good fellow, than I have given you for many a year! I'll raise your salary and endeavour to assist your struggling family, and we will discuss your affairs this very afternoon over a Christmas bowl of smoking bishop, Bob! Make up the fires and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

Bob stares, amazed. Scrooge smiles earnestly, claps him on the back.

INT. NARRATOR'S STUDY - NIGHT

The NARRATOR closes his book, looks at camera with satisfaction. The fire glows warmly.

NARRATOR
Scrooge was better than his word. He did it all, and infinitely more. To Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father. He became as good a friend, as good a master, and as good a man as the good old city knew. Some people laughed to see the alteration in him, but he let them laugh. His own heart laughed, and that was quite enough for him.

He lived upon the Total Abstinence Principle ever afterwards. It was always said of him that he knew how to keep Christmas well if any man alive possessed the knowledge. May that be truly said of us, and all of us!

The NARRATOR smiles.

NARRATOR (cont'd)
And so, as Tiny Tim observed, God bless Us, Every One!

FADE OUT.

THE END
